public class WordIndexFinder {
    private String inputString;

    public WordIndexFinder(String inputString) {
        this.inputString = inputString;
    }

    public void findWordIndices(String word) {
        if (inputString == null || word == null) {
            System.out.println("Invalid input!");
            return;
        }

        int index = inputString.indexOf(word);
        if (index == -1) {
            System.out.println("The word " + word + " is not in the string.");
            return;
        }

        int firstIndex = index;
        int lastIndex = index;
        while (index != -1) {
            lastIndex = index;
            index = inputString.indexOf(word, index + 1);
        }

        System.out.println("The first occurrence of " + word + " is at index " + firstIndex + ".");
        System.out.println("The last occurrence of " + word + " is at index " + lastIndex + ".");
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String inputString = "The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog.";
        String word = "the";
        WordIndexFinder finder = new WordIndexFinder(inputString);
        finder.findWordIndices(word);
    }
}
```

Explanation:

This program defines a class `WordIndexFinder` with a constructor that takes an input string and a method `findWordIndices` that takes a word and output the index of the first and last occurrence of the word in the string.

In the `findWordIndices` method, we first check if the input string and word are null. If either of them is null, we print an error message and return early. Otherwise, we use the `indexOf` method of the String class to find the index of the first occurrence of the word in the string. If the index is -1, it means the word is not in the string, so we print an appropriate message and return.

If the index is not -1, we initialize two variables `firstIndex` and `lastIndex` to the same value as `index`. Then we use a while loop to find the index of the next occurrence of the word, starting from `index + 1`. We update `lastIndex` to the new index, and continue the loop until `indexOf` returns -1.

Finally, we print the first and last indices of the word in the string.

The `main` method provides an example of using the `WordIndexFinder` class with some sample input.

