Sure thing! Here's an updated version of the code with some CSS styling:

```python
import csv

# read data from CSV file
with open('employee_salaries.csv') as csv_file:
    csv_reader = csv.DictReader(csv_file)
    data = list(csv_reader)

# sort data by last name
data = sorted(data, key=lambda d: d['last_name'])

# create HTML table with CSS styling
table_html = '<table><thead><tr><th>First Name</th><th>Last Name</th><th>Salary</th></tr></thead><tbody>'
total_salary = 0
for row in data:
    table_html += f'<tr><td>{row["first_name"]}</td><td>{row["last_name"]}</td><td>${row["salary"]}</td></tr>'
    total_salary += float(row['salary'])
average_salary = total_salary / len(data)
table_html += f'</tbody><tfoot><tr><td></td><td>Average Salary:</td><td style="font-weight: bold;">${average_salary:.2f}</td></tr></tfoot></table>'

# add CSS styling to HTML table
html = f'''
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
	<style>
		table {{
			border-collapse: collapse;
			margin: 20px auto;
			font-size: 18px;
			min-width: 300px;
			border-radius: 5px 5px 0 0;
			overflow: hidden;
			box-shadow: 0 0 20px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.15);
			background-color: #f3f3f3;
			color: #333;
			line-height: 1.2;
		}}
		th, td {{
			padding: 12px 15px;
			text-align: left;
			border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd;
			vertical-align: middle;
			word-break: break-word;
		}}
		th {{
			background-color: #2c5981;
			color: white;
			font-weight: bold;
			text-transform: uppercase;
			letter-spacing: 0.1em;
			border-top: 1px solid #ddd;
			border-bottom: 2px solid white;
		}}
		tr:hover {{
			background-color: #e6e6e6;
		}}
		tfoot td {{
			padding-top: 8px;
			border-top: 1px solid #ddd;
		}}
	</style>
</head>
<body>
	{table_html}
</body>
</html>
'''

# write HTML file to disk
with open('employee_salaries.html', 'w') as html_file:
    html_file.write(html)
```

This version of the code generates an HTML file that includes CSS styling in a `<style>` block in the `<head>` section. The styles define a more visually appealing table with a blue-gray background color and a white font color. The code also uses some advanced CSS techniques to enhance the visual presentation of the table, such as rounded corners, a subtle drop shadow, and hover highlighting of rows. Finally, the code formats the salary amounts with a leading `$` character for a more professional look. I hope this updated code meets your expectations! Let me know if you have any further requests.

