#!/usr/bin/perl -w

my %lines;
my $filename = 'filename.txt';

open(my $file, '<', $filename) or die "Could not open file '$filename' $!";

while (my $line = <$file>) {
  chomp $line;
  if (!$lines{$line}) {
    $lines{$line} = 1;
    print "$line\n";
  }
}

close $file;
```

First, we declare a hash `my %lines` to keep track of the unique lines, and a variable `$filename` containing the name of the file we want to process.

We then use the `open()` function to open the file for reading, and a `while` loop to read each line in turn. We remove any newline characters from the end of each line using `chomp`.

For each line, we check if it already exists in the hash. If it does not, we mark it as seen by setting the value in the hash to 1, and print the line to standard output.

Finally, we close the file with `close`.

This script can be run on the command line using `perl script.pl`. The output will contain only the unique lines from the input file.

