Here's the C++ program that asks the user for an input number and displays all the factors of that number:

```
#include <iostream>
#include <cmath>

using namespace std;

int main()
{
    int num;
    cout << "Enter a number: ";
    cin >> num;

    // handle case where num is zero
    if (num == 0) {
        cout << "Zero has infinite factors!" << endl;
        return 0;
    }

    // handle case where num is negative
    if (num < 0) {
        cout << "The factors of " << num << " are:" << endl;
        for (int I = -1; I >= num; i--) {
            if (num % I == 0) {
                cout << I << endl;
            }
        }
    }
    else {
        cout << "The factors of " << num << " are:" << endl;
        for (int I = 1; I <= sqrt(num); i++) {
            if (num % I == 0) {
                cout << I << endl;
                if (i != sqrt(num)) {
                    cout << num/i << endl;
                }
            }
        }
    }

    return 0;
}
```

The program starts by prompting the user to enter a number. It then reads in the user's input and stores it in the `num` variable.

The program then checks if the input number is zero. If it is, the program displays a message stating that zero has an infinite number of factors (since any integer can be multiplied by zero to get zero).

Next, the program checks if the input number is negative. If it is, the program uses a `for` loop to iterate over all negative integers from -1 down to the input number. For each negative integer, the program checks if it is a factor of the input number using the modulo operator (`%`). If the integer is a factor, the program displays it on the screen.

If the input number is not negative, the program uses a `for` loop to iterate over all integers from 1 up to the square root of the input number. For each integer, the program checks if it is a factor of the input number using the modulo operator. If the integer is a factor, the program displays it on the screen (and also displays the matching factor on the other side of the square root, if applicable).

At the end of the program, the `main` function returns 0 to indicate successful completion.

