```
import java.util.InputMismatchException;
import java.util.Scanner;

public class BinarySearch {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner input = new Scanner(System.in);

        try {
            // Prompt user to input array of integers in ascending order
            System.out.print("Enter the number of elements in the array: ");
            int n = input.nextInt();

            int[] arr = new int[n];
            System.out.print("Enter the elements of the array: ");
            for (int I = 0; I < n; i++) {
                arr[i] = input.nextInt();
            }

            // Prompt user to input value to search for
            System.out.print("Enter the value to search for: ");
            int val = input.nextInt();

            // Initialize variables for binary search
            int left = 0;
            int right = n - 1;
            int mid;

            // Binary Search Algorithm
            while (left <= right) {
                mid = (left + right) / 2;

                if (arr[mid] == val) {
                    System.out.println("Value found at index " + mid);
                    return;
                } else if (arr[mid] < val) {
                    left = mid + 1;
                } else {
                    right = mid - 1;
                }
            }

            // If value not found in array
            System.out.println("Value not found in array.");
        } catch (InputMismatchException e) {
            System.out.println("Invalid input. Please enter integers only.");
        }
    }
}
```

This will catch any `InputMismatchException` that is thrown when attempting to read an integer from the user. If an exception is caught, the program will display an error message and exit.

