Sure thing! Here's an example of a nested `for` loop that involves arrays:

```c++
#include <iostream>

using namespace std;

int main() {
   int arr[3][3] = {{1, 2, 3},
                    {4, 5, 6},
                    {7, 8, 9}};

   // Loop through rows
   for(int I = 0; I < 3; i++) {
      // Loop through columns
      for(int j = 0; j < 3; j++) {
         cout << arr[i][j] << " ";
      }
      cout << endl;
   }

   return 0;
}
```

In this example, we're using a 2D array `arr` with 3 rows and 3 columns. We've initialized it with some values using the following syntax:

```c++
int arr[3][3] = {{1, 2, 3},
                 {4, 5, 6},
                 {7, 8, 9}};
```

This creates an array with 3 rows and 3 columns, and sets the values of each element as shown.

The nested `for` loop in the `main()` function starts by looping through each row of the array `arr` (`for(int I = 0; I < 3; i++)`). Within this loop, we use another loop to iterate through each column, and print out the value of the element at that position (`cout << arr[i][j] << " ";`).

Essentially, an array is a collection of variables of the same type, which are stored in contiguous memory locations. Each element in an array can be accessed using an index, which starts from 0 and goes up to one less than the size of the array.

For example, in the array `int arr[5] = { 10, 20, 30, 40, 50 };`, `arr[0]` is the first element, which has a value of 10, `arr[1]` is the second element with a value of 20, and so on.

Similarly, a 2D array is essentially an array of arrays. Each element of a 2D array can be accessed using two indices, one for the row and one for the column.

In the example above, `arr[0][0]` is the element in the first row and first column, which has a value of 1, `arr[0][1]` is the element in the first row and second column, which has a value of 2, and so on.

