Assistant: ```
#include<iostream>
using namespace std;
int binarySearch(int arr[], int l, int r, int x)
{
    while (l <= r) {
        int mid = l + (r - l) / 2;
        if (arr[mid] == x)
            return mid;
        if (arr[mid] < x)
            l = mid + 1;
        else
            r = mid - 1;
    }
    return -1;
}
int main()
{
    int arr[] = { 2, 5, 7, 10, 14, 16, 19 };
    int n = sizeof(arr) / sizeof(arr[0]);
    int x = 10;
    int result = binarySearch(arr, 0, n - 1, x);
    if (result == -1)
        cout << "Element is not present in array" << endl;
    else
        cout << "Element found at index " << result << endl;
    return 0;
}
```

In this program, the binarySearch() function takes a sorted array 'arr', the left
and right indices of the sub-array being searched, and the value 'x' to be found.
It returns the index of 'x' if present, or returns -1 if not present. 

We start the search by setting 'l' and 'r' to the first and last indices of the array, 
respectively. We calculate the middle index 'mid' in each iteration and check if 
'arr[mid]' is equal to 'x'. If it is, we just return the index 'mid'. If 'arr[mid]' 
is less than 'x', we set the new left index 'l' to 'mid+1' to discard the left 
sub-array. Otherwise, we set the new right index 'r' to 'mid-1' to discard the right 
sub-array.

In the main() function, we create an example array 'arr' and find the index of 'x'
using the binarySearch() function. If the result is -1, element 'x' is not present, 
so we print a message to that effect. Otherwise, we print the index at which 'x' is found.

This program implements the iterative approach of binary search algorithm, which has 
a time complexity of O(log n) and space complexity of O(1).

