```
#!/usr/bin/perl

use strict;
use warnings;

# search pattern
my $pattern = "example";

# directory to search in
my $dir = '/path/to/directory';

opendir(my $dh, $dir) || die "Can't open directory: $!";

while (my $file = readdir $dh) {
    # skip files that aren't readable or aren't files
    next unless (-f "$dir/$file" && -r "$dir/$file");

    # search for pattern in file contents
    if (`grep -q '$pattern' "$dir/$file"`) {
        my $new_name = "$dir/$file.$pattern";
        rename("$dir/$file", $new_name) || die "File rename failed: $!";
        print "Renamed $dir/$file to $new_name\n";
    }
}

closedir $dh;
```

Note: This script uses the `grep` command to search for the pattern in the file contents. This may not be the most efficient or accurate way to search for a pattern, and may not work as expected if the pattern includes special characters. A more robust solution would use a Perl module such as `File::Find` and `File::Grep` to search for the pattern in a more reliable way.

